changelog: |
  0.3.0 — a lane copied from the template is born on the harness standard
  (_data/fleet.yml `harnesses.standard`). The caller template now passes a
  turn cap and a matching dollar cap (`max-turns: "120"`, `max-budget-usd:
  "20"`, the hub evolution loop's own sizing for the same job), and its cron
  is a placeholder (`__CRON__`) instead of an example. The 0.2.x shape had no
  turn cap, so every lane made from it was one control short, and twelve
  lanes kept its example cron (`17 6 * * 1`) and so shared Monday 06:17 UTC.
  A caller is a file the repo holds and fills in, so existing lanes change by
  a PR in their own repo, not by a fan-out. Test: test_harness_registry.py
  holds a filled template on the standard.
  The runtime changes in one place: a TURN-CAP STOP is final, like a dollar-cap
  stop. run.sh classified only the dollar cap as a circuit breaker, so
  `error_max_turns` fell through to the single-shot API fallback, and where a
  consumer ships one (it-journey, zer0-mistakes) the step ended GREEN on one
  completion while the agent's work was cut off. It now exits 1 with the
  reason and an ::error:: naming --max-turns. Consumed by reference (@main),
  so every lane gets this on merge: a lane that was hitting its turn cap and
  passing turns red, which is the point. Contract: tests/contract.sh cases
  15-16.
  And one input is new: `permission-mode` (claude-run, ai-lane.yml, run.sh
  --permission-mode / AI_PERMISSION_MODE): acceptEdits (the default, and what
  the runner always passed), default, or plan. A read-only reviewer moved from
  a bare `claude -p` onto claude-run was handed acceptEdits, which approves
  file edits its tool allowlist never granted; `permission-mode: default`
  keeps the allowlist as the whole grant. Any other mode is refused before the
  CLI runs. Contract: case 7b (33 cases in all).
  0.2.1 — an empty `model:` in the consumer's _data/ai.yml is the fleet default.
  The kit seeds `model: ""` with the comment "Empty = fleet default", and the
  resolver returned that empty string (`"" || default` is "" in Ruby), so the
  CLI was called with `--model ""`: twelve evolve lanes carried it on
  2026-11-10. A null or missing value already fell through. Consumed by
  reference (@main), so every lane gets this with no fan-out. Contract:
  tests/contract.sh case 14 (six model-resolution cases).
  0.2.0 — the credential ORDER is configuration. run.sh reads AI_AUTH_ORDER
  (ai-lane.yml passes the caller repo's CLAUDE_AUTH_ORDER; CLAUDE_AUTH_ORDER is
  read directly when AI_AUTH_ORDER is unset), gives the CLI ONLY the credential
  being tried, and — new — moves to the next method when a call is REFUSED
  (auth rejected or quota exhausted), never after any other failure, so a task
  is not re-run once it has spent turns. An order without api_key never spends
  the key, not even on the single-shot API fallback. Hints name the credential
  that actually failed. ai-lane's gate counts only credentials the order uses.
  Unset order = oauth,api_key = the 0.1.0 behaviour. Consumed by reference
  (@main), so every lane gets this with no fan-out; the vendored scripts/ai/run.sh
  copies (lifehacker.dev, it-journey, zer0-mistakes) are now behind and should be
  re-copied. Contract: tests/contract.sh cases 8-12.
  0.1.0 — the fleet's model step and lane, versioned ONCE. Promoted from
  lifehacker.dev scripts/ai/run.sh + .github/actions/claude-run (PR #643), where
  a real fleet of 17 lanes tests it daily, after the 2026-09-06 review found
  three divergent copies of one `claude-run` contract (lifehacker, it-journey,
  zer0-mistakes — three hashes, one name) and two of them exiting 0 on a dead
  credential. The runner: model resolution (--model > AI_MODEL > the consumer's
  _data/ai.yml > fleet default), OAuth-first (`env -u ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`),
  Claude Code primary with an optional consumer-owned API fallback, optional
  consumer-owned metering, a post-run one-paragraph-per-line normalizer honouring
  the consumer's .prose-excludes, and the exit contract: 0 = ran or nothing was
  attempted, 1 = attempted and FAILED (with the reason, as a ::error::). The
  reusable lane adds the kill switch (`vars.<SWITCH>`, dispatch bypasses), the
  bot guard, named concurrency, probed GH_PAT → GH_TOKEN, runtime setup,
  pre/post hooks, and the result-file assertion. Ten contract tests.
notes: |
  Consumed by reference, so there is nothing for --upgrade to byte-compare:
  every consumer follows @main on its next run. The caller template IS a file a
  repo holds, so it is stamped and archived like agent-context; the runtime is
  not. Ruby is the only runtime the runner needs beyond bash (model resolution,
  result parsing) and ubuntu-latest ships it. Consumers that vendored the
  runner (scripts/ai/run.sh) prove parity with
  `AI_RUNNER_SUT=scripts/ai/run.sh bash tests/contract.sh`.
